---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedPlaybooks from '../../components/playbooks/RelatedPlaybooks.astro';

const today = '2026-07-04T00:00:00+00:00';

const faqs = [
  { q: `What is the Best Answer Hub guide to where to start with AI?`, a: `Best Answer Hub's starting method is deliberately small: find the single weekly task that wastes the most time, test one free or low-cost AI tool against it for 30 days, and keep the tool only if you can measure a real gain. It is vendor-neutral, sells nothing, and is built for a 1 to 50 person business with no technical team.` },
  { q: `Where should a small business start with AI?`, a: `Start with a task, not a tool. Pick one repetitive weekly job that wastes real time and has a low cost of being wrong, such as drafting routine emails or summarizing notes. Test a single tool against it for a month. This keeps spend tiny and ties every decision to a result you can see.` },
  { q: `Am I too late to start using AI?`, a: `No. Most small businesses have barely begun. US government data shows only about 8.8% of small firms use AI to produce their goods or services, so the field is wide open. Starting now with one well-chosen task puts you ahead of most peers, not behind them.` },
  { q: `What is the best first AI use case for a small business?`, a: `The best first use case is a high-volume, low-stakes task you already understand. Common winners are drafting routine content, summarizing meetings or documents, sorting and replying to repetitive email, and first-draft marketing copy. These let you check the output quickly and feel the time saved within days, not months.` },
  { q: `How much does it cost to start using AI?`, a: `Very little. Entry-level AI subscriptions typically run $20 to $30 a month, and the median monthly entry cost for small businesses fell about 60% between 2019 and 2024. Many capable tools have free tiers, so a first test can cost nothing beyond the time to run it.` },
  { q: `Do I have to pay for AI tools to get started?`, a: `No. About half of small firms using AI report no spending on it at all. Most leading tools offer free tiers that are enough to test a single task. Start free, prove the task works, and only pay once a tool has earned it by saving measurable time.` },
  { q: `Which AI tool should a beginner choose first?`, a: `Choose the tool that fits the one task you picked, not the most advertised brand. For writing and summarizing, a general assistant with a free tier is enough to start. The US Small Business Administration's advice is to test free or low-cost tools first and keep only what adds value.` },
  { q: `How do I pick the right first task for AI?`, a: `Score candidate tasks against five traits: it happens weekly, it has low stakes if wrong, you can check the output in minutes, it needs no confidential data, and it clearly eats hours today. A task that ticks all five is a strong first pick. One that fails several is best left for later.` },
  { q: `What is the 30-day AI test?`, a: `The 30-day test is a simple rule: run one AI tool against one task for a month, then decide. If you can measure a clear improvement, such as hours saved or fewer errors, keep it. If you cannot, drop the tool and try the next task. It keeps spend small and tied to evidence.` },
  { q: `How do I measure whether an AI tool is actually working?`, a: `Measure two things before and after: the time the task takes and the quality of the result. Controlled studies show AI can speed up well-defined tasks meaningfully, but the gain is only real for your business if you can see it. Time saved becomes value only when it is reused on paid or growth work.` },
  { q: `What mistakes do small businesses make when starting with AI?`, a: `The four most common are buying a tool before defining the problem, trying to roll AI out everywhere at once, sending unchecked AI output to customers, and typing sensitive data into free public tools. Starting with one task, one tool, and a human check avoids all four.` },
  { q: `Do I need technical skills or a developer to start with AI?`, a: `No. Most small businesses start with ready-made tools that need no code. The skill that matters is judgment: writing a clear instruction, checking the output, and knowing when it is wrong. That is a habit any team can build, not a technical role you need to hire for.` },
  { q: `Does my data need to be ready before I start with AI?`, a: `Not all of it. You do not need a big data cleanup to start. Pick a first task that needs little or no sensitive data, so you can test quickly and safely. Organize data later, and only the data a specific, proven use case actually needs.` },
  { q: `What should I do after my first AI win?`, a: `Repeat the method on the next task rather than buying a bigger platform. Add tasks one at a time, write a simple one-page policy once more than one person is using AI, and check your wider readiness across data, tools, process, skills, and governance before you try to scale.` },
  { q: `How is this different from a vendor's "getting started with AI" guide?`, a: `Most vendor guides are built to funnel you toward the maker's own product, so every step ends at a purchase. The Best Answer Hub method sells no tools, carries no affiliate links, and is happy to tell you to start free or wait. The first win is the goal, not a sale.` },
];

const seo = {
  title: 'Where to Start With AI: A Small Business Guide (2026)',
  description: 'Where should a small business start with AI? Find the weekly task that wastes the most time and test one tool against it for 30 days before spending anything.',
  url: '/playbooks/where-to-start-with-ai/',
  type: 'WebPage' as const,
  datePublished: today,
  dateModified: today,
